> # ================================================================
> # Task 35 - Direction-split agreement and precision / recall
> # Date: Sep 3, 2026
> #
> # Closes plan.md step 7b item 3, raised in the 2026-08-28 supervisor
> # review and restated in REVIEW.md Q11.
> #
> # THE COMPLAINT
> #
> # results.md section 6 compares the flagging methods with Cohen's
> # kappa and with agreement conditional on being flagged by either.
> # Both collapse a 3 x 3 outcome - hypo / none / hyper - into
> # flagged / not, which throws away the most informative case. Two
> # methods that flag the same cell in OPPOSITE directions are counted
> # as agreeing under a flagged/not view, and as a plain disagreement
> # under kappa. They should be counted as the strongest possible
> # disagreement, because one of them must be wrong.
> #
> # Kappa is also unreliable here: with >95% zeros it is dominated by
> # the marginal flag rates and moves for reasons unrelated to
> # agreement (REVIEW.md Q11).
> #
> # WHAT THIS DOES INSTEAD
> #
> # The full 3 x 3 contingency for every method pair, then:
> #
> #   jaccard.pos / jaccard.neg   agreement computed within one
> #                               direction at a time, so a hyper
> #                               agreement is never propped up by a
> #                               hypo one
> #   opposite                    cells where A says +1 and B says -1,
> #                               or the reverse. Reported as a share
> #                               of the cells either method flagged.
> #   precision / recall          taking `ext` as the designated
> #                               reference, as the review asked:
> #                               precision = of the cells method A
> #                               flags, what share does ext flag in
> #                               the SAME direction; recall = of the
> #                               cells ext flags, what share does A
> #                               recover. Direction is required to
> #                               match, so a hyper-for-hypo swap
> #                               counts against both.
> #
> # All of it per methylation state as well as pooled, and at both
> # scales - the 100-CpG window from the email and the whole
> # chromosome - because section 6's numbers are window-only and the
> # window is not representative (results.md section 5).
> #
> # Every method except `ext` is a within-cohort rule at n = 53 and so
> # is degenerate by construction (README, "the self-reference
> # degeneracy"). The numbers below are still worth having: they say
> # HOW the methods disagree, which is a different question from
> # whether any of them is right.
> # ================================================================
> 
